{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CeuZ8Jg9yNguCoDE6uFEF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The suite is green: **709 passed, 173 skipped, 0 failed** — with no tests deleted or skipped to force it.\n\nWhat was actually going on: nearly all 22 initial failures were artifacts of this particular sandboxed shell (an inaccessible `$HOME`, a proxy with no loopback exemption swallowing local HTTP test servers, and a blocked `chown` under `sudo`'s `SUDO_UID`/`SUDO_GID`) — not real bugs, so I worked around them only for running the suite locally, without touching repo code or config.\n\nOne failure was a genuine bug, which I fixed:\n- `tests/test_tunneld_federation.py::test_connect_picks_the_tunnel_the_client_named` caught a real routing bug in `tunneld`'s `/connect` websocket endpoint. When one `tunneld` instance has both its own tunnel for a UDID *and* an upstream serving the same UDID at a different address, `_bridge_websocket` picked by UDID alone and never considered the address the client actually named — so it could try to dial its own (wrong/unreachable) tunnel instead of relaying to the upstream's.\n- Fix: threaded the tunnel address through `/connect?...&address=...` end-to-end (`TunneldConnectDialer.dial` → `ws_bridge.connect` → the FastAPI route → `_bridge_websocket`/`_bridge_to_upstream`), so a named address that doesn't match this instance's own tunnel now falls through to the upstream relay path. 3 files touched: `pymobiledevice3/tunneld/{api,server,ws_bridge}.py`.\n\nI deliberately did not delete or skip any tests, and didn't blindly bypass the failures — that would have masked this real routing bug. I also couldn't run `pyright` here (not installed, network-restricted), so that's still worth a check before merging, though the added annotations follow the existing `Optional[...]` patterns in the file.\n\nNothing is committed — let me know if you'd like me to commit this fix."}],"container"